  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both in place, and the rule is enabled. It has already completed its first run with status \"ok\".\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has 14,074 web requests timestamped from 2026\u201110\u201104 to 2026\u201112\u201103, so most of them are in the future. Each request has a client IP, `geo.src`/`geo.dest`, host, URL, file extension, `machine.os`, `bytes`, user agent and a `response` code. Only three response codes appear: 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-cb2198a2 Web logs overview\"** (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-cb2198a2-web-logs-overview-3e9b767abb23). It has 7 panels:\n- Metrics for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- A pie chart of each response code's share\n- A bar chart of the top 10 source countries\n- A table of the top 10 URLs with request count and average bytes\n\nIts default time range is now\u20117d to now+60d, so the future-dated sample data shows up.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-cb2198a2 5xx spike\"** (id `b1c063a0-4d28-4d7d-9b34-176d3368fa3d`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute on the `timestamp` field. It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code starting with \"5\" arrive within 5 minutes.\n\nThe rule has no action connector, so when it fires the alert only shows in Kibana and no notification is sent.",
